I received several responses, my thanks to :

Kevin Criss
Michael Acklin
Pat O'Brien
Richard Loken
Alan Nabeth

for taking their time to respond.

Highlights from the responses :

Legato works pretty well.
Gbit ethernet shortens backup time significantly.
Great Lakes Computer (616)698-1100/Ryan is an alternate source for
pre-printed labels.
If you print your own labels, use durable ink on stiff stock to avoid
registration problems.
MRU (robot) or mcutil will query and display the barcode label

I've had a couple days to play with the unit and I'm pretty pleased. My
backup times have dropped significantly due partly to the faster tape drives
and partly due to having the new mini-library on a separate SCSI bus. My old
TZ89 was on the same HSZ50 as the disks it was backing up, causing
significant I/O contention.

For now I still plan on enhancing my existing backup scripts to manage the
library. I'll add a dbm or perl database of tape labels to log first and
last used dates. This will allow me to verify tape rotation schedules and
flag a tape for replacement in the rotation.

There are a number of bar-code packages available commercially with varying
UNIX support. The gnu-barcode project and foobar are free.

http://www.allen-sauer.com/oracle-toolbox/unix-foobar

http://www.linux.org/apps/AppId_2208.html

Links to many UNIX barcode resources can be found here :

http://www.adams1.com/pub/russadam/unix.html

Alan Davis
davis@tessco.com
